    "result_text": "The full build passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec requires the create-owner response (and subsequent GETs) to return `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners that already shared the same `firstName` + `lastName` (case-insensitively) *before* this create. Since the acceptance test reads it back via a separate `GET /api/owners/{id}`, the value has to be **computed at create time and persisted** on the owner.\n\nI followed the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` pattern exactly:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added `namesake_count INTEGER` to the `owners` table in all four dialects (h2, hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, postgres, mysql).\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` (readOnly int32, min 0) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and MapStruct maps it automatically by name (no mapper change needed).\n\n4. **New function `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount`** \u2014 a single-responsibility step that counts existing owners with a matching first+last name (case-insensitive, null-safe), computed before save so it never counts the new owner itself.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n## Verification\n- The new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` passes (second same-named owner reports `namesakeCount == 1`).\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, no regressions.\n\nThe counting logic lives in its own small function class rather than being bolted onto an existing step, keeping each function focused, and it reuses the same `OwnerRepository.findAll()` + normalized-comparison idiom already used by the duplicate-check and customer-code steps.",
